(* ============================================================================
   PART 2: BLOCK ELEMENTS
   ============================================================================ *)

(* --- Headings --- *)
(* ATX (`#` prefix) only. Setext (underline) headings are intentionally
   NOT supported, matching djot: a `---` underline collides with the
   thematic break and frontmatter delimiter, reintroducing the ambiguity
   djot removed and breaking Design Principle 1 ("one syntax, one
   meaning"). *)
heading = atx_heading ;

atx_heading = heading_first_line ;
heading_first_line = heading_marker, space+, inline_content, newline ;
heading_marker = '#' | "##" | "###" | "####" | "#####" | "######" ;
(* A HEADING MARKER STARTS AT ITS CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-001]. A
   heading_marker is classified in its owner's coordinates (PART 0 S3,
   CARVE-P0-024): at or past the owner's content column, at every level, it
   opens a heading. *)

(* THE HEADING MARKER SEPARATOR IS A RUN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-002]. `space+`: one or more
   ASCII spaces after the hashes, ALL of them separator, and the first
   character that is not one BEGINS the heading. `##<SP><SP>h` is the heading
   `h`, not `<SP>h`.

   TWO CLAUSES DECIDE IT: CARVE-P7-002 lists four
   exactly-one-space padding slots, a CLOSED SET excluding this separator. PART 11 §1 lets
   `fmt` normalize marker alignment: `##<SP><SP>h` becomes `##<SP>h`.

   A TAB IS NOT SEPARATOR. `space = ' '`, so `#<SP><TAB>x` is a heading whose
   text BEGINS with the tab, and `#<TAB>x` is not a heading at all -- the
   run is ASCII spaces and the first character that is not one ends it. This
   is the same answer the caption gives at the same position.

   AND THE MARKER REQUIRES CONTENT after the run as well: hashes, the
   separator and nothing but whitespace open no heading, and the line is
   ordinary paragraph text with its trailing run dropped.

   WHAT MOVES WITH THE TEXT, AND WHAT DOES NOT. The derived id does NOT
   change: slugging drops a leading run either way, so `##<SP><SP>a b` is
   `a-b`. A CROSSREF's AUTO-TEXT takes the heading's own text (CARVE-P9-039), so
   `</#a-b>` renders `a b`. *)

(* SINGLE-LINE HEADINGS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-003] (diverges from Djot). A heading ENDS AT
   THE NEWLINE. It has no continuation line, and nothing following it folds in:
   the next line begins whatever block it begins, exactly as it would after any
   other closed block.

     # Title          -> <h1>Title</h1> then <p>Some text.</p>
     Some text.

     ## A             -> two separate <h2> elements
     ## B

   A `^ ` caption line is no exception: it does not fold in, and it does not
   attach either, because a heading is NOT one of PART 9 §4's captionable hosts. It
   opens an ordinary paragraph of its own.

     # H              -> <h1>H</h1> then <p>^ cap</p>
     ^ cap

   NO TRAILING ATTRIBUTES (djot-strict). A heading line carries NO trailing
   `{...}` attribute block: a `{...}` at the end of a heading line is ordinary
   inline content (literal text unless it forms an inline construct), and the
   id derives from the full literal text. Attributes attach via a PRECEDING
   block-attribute line (PART 9 §15), the uniform block rule; an explicit
   `#id` from that line lives on the `<section>` wrapper (PART 9 §13). *)

(* HEADING IDENTIFIERS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-004]. A heading without an explicit {#id} gets an
   automatic identifier from its TEXT CONTENT, GitHub/SSG-style. WHICH INLINES
   CONTRIBUTE IS PART OF THE RULE: an inline contributes
   the literal text it carries, or it contributes nothing, and which of the two
   is a property of the CONSTRUCT rather than of what it renders -- an id is
   assigned before a cross-reference resolves or a symbol is looked up. Code
   spans, MATH runs, an image's alt text, a link's label and a superscript
   contribute; raw inline content for every `{=format}` target, footnote
   references, cross-references, symbols `:name:` and editorial comments
   `{# #}` do not, and a line comment ends the line it sits on. A comment is
   about the text rather than part of it, so adding or removing one never
   moves an id: `# x {#c#} y` gets `id="x-y"`. The heading index (PART 9R R1)
   and a task box's `aria-label` (CARVE-P2-015) read the same text, so
   `[x y][]` finds that heading and `- [ ] x {#c#} y` is named `x y`.
   Then, on that text:
     - reverse smart-typography output to its ASCII source first, so the id does
       not depend on presentation (`Don’t` -> `Don-t`, `Step 1 → 2` -> `Step-1-2`).
       Paired quotation marks reverse together: `‘`, `’`, `‚`, `‛` to ASCII
       apostrophe, and `“`, `”`, `„`, `‟`, `«`, `»` to ASCII double quote;
     - replace each maximal run of non-alphanumeric ASCII characters with a single
       `-` (covers spaces, punctuation, `_`, and runs of `-`) -- the jgm/djot#393 rule;
     - trim leading/trailing `-`;
     - PRESERVE CASE (Unicode-aware): NON-ASCII characters AND letter case pass
       through unchanged (`Café` -> `Café`, `日本語` unchanged). The text IS
       NFC-normalized before slugging (CARVE-P9-058), so a precomposed `é` and a
       decomposed `e` + U+0301 give the SAME id;
     - a result that starts with a Unicode number (`\p{N}`, not only an ASCII
       digit: `# ½ cup` gives `s-½-cup`) is prefixed `s-` (a bare leading digit
       is a valid HTML id but an invalid CSS selector); an empty result becomes
       `s`;
     - duplicates within a document get a numeric `-2`, `-3`, ... suffix. Only
       an id the document renders takes part: an id dropped with an unresolved
       reference's attributes (PART 9R) reserves nothing, so `x[^n]{#i}` above
       `## {#i}` leaves the heading `id="i"` (carve#4288).
   An explicit {#id} is used verbatim (case preserved). Implementations MAY
   offer OPT-IN, orthogonal lowercasing and ASCII-folding transforms; neither
   is the default. ASCII-folding has TWO modes: BEST-EFFORT keeps characters
   its shared transliteration table cannot map, while STRICT drops that residue
   so the id matches `[0-9A-Za-z-]`. The table is not normative, but engines
   MUST use the same table. Cross-references match the id table EXACTLY, case
   included (PART 9R R4). *)

(* MARKDOWN MIGRATION FRAGMENTS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-031]. An importer
   rewriting an inline Markdown link destination's local fragment, within one file or a
   migrated directory, MUST first look for an exact id in the converted
   target's rendered HTML. If none exists and lowercasing the fragment and the
   converted target's heading ids finds exactly one heading id, it MUST replace
   the fragment with that id in its Carve case. Multiple
   different heading ids with the same lowercase key remain unresolved, as do
   fragments with no match. To avoid confusing Markdown's duplicate heading
   suffixes with Carve's different numbering, a heading id ending in `-N`
   where N is at least 2 has no case-insensitive alias. An exact fragment still
   resolves to such an id. Non-heading ids have no case-insensitive match.
   A directory migration MUST report a rewritten target as rewritten and an ambiguous target as
   unresolved. Each link whose fragment changes this way MUST also add one
   `link-fragment-case-rewritten` row to the migration report that covers its
   file: `info` severity, `normalized` fidelity, `inferred` confidence, a
   `path` of `link:` followed by the destination as the link wrote it before
   the rewrite, and the message `Rewrote link fragment #OLD to #NEW, the one
   heading id it matches without regard to case.` with both fragments as the
   destinations spell them. The path and `#OLD` keep the author's spelling,
   not a percent-encoded form of it: `[a](#über-uns)` reports
   `link:#über-uns`. The importer keeps a destination as written, so
   `#über-uns` and `#%C3%BCber-uns` stay two spellings with two rows.
   `#NEW` is the fragment as the importer writes it into the rewritten
   link, non-ASCII included: `[a](#über-uns)` becomes `[a](#Über-Uns)`.
   Reference definitions and raw HTML URLs remain for manual review. This
   import-time repair does not change Carve's case-sensitive link or
   cross-reference resolution. *)

(* UNRESOLVED MARKDOWN REFERENCES KEEP THEIR SYNTAX ON IMPORT -- NORMATIVE
   [CARVE-P2-032]. A Markdown importer MUST write an unresolved link reference
   or footnote reference as a Carve reference, without escaping its opener.
   The rule applies in every inline position, including headings and task
   items. The reference remains available for later definition resolution. *)

(* --- Thematic Breaks --- *)
thematic_break = (('-', '-', '-', {'-'}) | ('*', '*', '*', {'*'}) | ('_', '_', '_', {'_'})), newline ;

(* A BARE `---` BLOCK IS IMPORTED AS FRONTMATTER ONLY WHERE IT SHAPES AS A
   MAPPING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-030]. A Markdown importer meeting a BARE
   `---` block at byte 0 of its SOURCE writes Carve frontmatter only where the
   test below calls the block a mapping. Everywhere else it writes the thematic
   break and the source lines for what they are -- under CommonMark, `Foo` over
   `---` is a setext heading -- in forms that cannot reopen frontmatter
   (PART 11 §6).

   A TYPED OPENER IS FRONTMATTER UNCONDITIONALLY, for every
   `frontmatter_format`: `---yaml`, `---toml`, `---json`, `---neon`. No shape
   test runs on one. `---yaml` / `Foo` / `---` is frontmatter whose payload is
   a scalar, which is the author's business and not the importer's.

   THE COLLISION IS THE BARE SPELLING'S ALONE, which is why the test is too. A
   break is a dash run and nothing else (PART 1), so a typed opener is never a
   thematic break and never a setext underline; with no closer ahead of it the
   line is ordinary paragraph text. Once the author named the format there is
   nothing left to infer.

   A BARE OPENER TAKES THE YAML TEST AND ONLY THAT ONE. PART 1 says a bare
   `---` defaults to `yaml`, so exactly one shape rule can apply to it. The
   consequence worth spelling: `---` / `[table]` / `---` is NOT frontmatter --
   `[table]` is no yaml mapping, and a toml rule does not reach a bare opener.

   THERE IS NO TOML SHAPE RULE. A typed `---toml` is frontmatter unconditionally
   and a bare opener is yaml, so a toml shape test has no reachable caller, and
   the shape test applies only to bare YAML openers.

   THE TEST. Take the lines between the opener and the closer. Skip blank lines
   and lines whose first non-space character is `#`. The block is frontmatter
   when the first remaining line, AT COLUMN 0, is a key followed by `:` and
   then a space, a tab or the end of the line. The key is a double-quoted or
   single-quoted string, or a run that starts with neither whitespace nor any
   of `-`, `[`, `{`, `"`, `'`, `#`, and holds no `:`.

   NO REMAINING LINE IS NOT A MAPPING, so an empty or comment-only block is not
   frontmatter either.

   COLUMN 0 IS A TEST ON THE FIRST REMAINING LINE, NOT A SECOND SKIP RULE. An
   indented key is that line and fails the test; only a blank line and a
   `#` line are ever skipped.

   This is a shape test. `title: [unclosed` has the required shape and
   therefore opens frontmatter despite malformed YAML content.

   THE GATE IS THE IMPORTER'S, NOT THE GRAMMAR'S. `frontmatter_content` is
   opaque lines (PART 1), so `---` / `Foo` / `---` is valid Carve frontmatter
   and nothing in the grammar separates it from a document's metadata. What an
   import owes is what the SOURCE means, and CommonMark has no frontmatter.

   EVERY CONVERSION IS REPORTED, bare or typed, whether or not it looks lossy:
   lines leave the document body for metadata a reader cannot see in the
   output. The code is `frontmatter-synthesized` on the importer's own
   migration report (`docs/format-bridges.md`), not a render loss -- a rendered
   document lost nothing. *)

(* --- Code Blocks --- *)
code_block = fenced_code_block, [caption_slot] ;
(* A trailing caption (PART 9 §4) wraps the code block in a figure: a
   numbered LISTING. The caption may carry a `#` number placeholder, and a
   `</#id>` to the block resolves to "Listing N" (PART 9 §19). *)

fenced_code_block = code_fence_open, [space], [code_fence_info], newline,
                    code_content,
                    code_fence_close, newline ;
(* The optional info-string padding is exactly one space (CARVE-P7-002).
   The glued form is canonical; ```` ```<TAB>php ```` and
   ```` ```<SP><SP>php ```` fall back to text. A trailing tab with no info
   token follows CARVE-P2-025. Raw-block selection follows PART 9 §20. *)
(* CODE-FENCE INFO STRING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-005]. After the optional language_info
   token the opener admits, in this fixed order, an optional "header" (a
   quoted_title -- the SAME token as the admonition header, PART 9 §12) and an
   optional bracketed [label]. Both are STRUCTURED METADATA -- neither is part
   of the language/class:
     - HEADER ("...") -- a human-visible title for the block. Carried by core
       as the `title` attribute on `<pre>`. The header uses the same quoted_title TOKEN as
       the admonition header, but because it targets an HTML attribute the text
       is LITERAL (not inline-parsed) -- only HTML-escaped -- so markup-like
       characters in a filename (`*.config.js`, `a_b.py`) survive intact. An
       empty "" is a supplied empty title (`title=""`).
     - LABEL ([...]) -- a short grouping identifier. The core renderer ignores
       it; a group extension (e.g. code-group) uses it as the tab label.
   A code fence still carries NO inline {...} attributes -- use the PRECEDING
   block-attribute line for class/id/data-* (PART 9 §15):
       {.fancy #x}
       ```php "src/Auth.php"
       ...
       ```
   which renders on the <pre> (language stays `language-…` on the <code>). If
   the preceding line ALSO sets `title`, the preceding line wins (the explicit
   attribute channel overrides the opener-header sugar).
   INVALID-FENCE FALLBACK: when the text after the language token is anything
   other than an optional "header" then an optional [label] in that order -- a
   bare second word, a key="value" pair (```js title="x"), an inline `{...}`
   block (``` php {.x}), or the wrong order (```php [l] "h") -- the line is NOT
   a fenced code block; it falls back to ordinary inline parsing (the backtick
   run typically opens an inline code span). Quotes and brackets are the only
   delimiters that admit metadata. The raw passthrough opener (```=FORMAT,
   PART 9 §20) is a SEPARATE production matched before this one (a leading `=`
   is never a language, and a raw block takes no header). *)
(* AN IMPORTER DROPS INFO-STRING TEXT THAT IS NOT A LANGUAGE -- NORMATIVE
   [CARVE-P2-029]. A Markdown info string is free-form, so a source may carry
   text that is neither a `language_info` token nor one of the two metadata
   slots above (```` ```ruby startline=3 ````). The importer DROPS that text.
   An unrecognized token carries no defined meaning, so every structured slot
   it could be written into is an arbitrary choice. *)

code_fence_open  = (backtick_fence | tilde_fence):open ;
code_fence_close = (backtick_fence | tilde_fence):close
                   where char(close) = char(open) and len(close) >= len(open) ;
(* FORMAL (PART 9 §2, stated as a guard): the closer uses the SAME fence
   character as its opener and is at least as long. The `where` clause is the
   grammar's counting device -- see GUARD NOTATION in the header. *)
(* COLUMN-EXACT DELIMITERS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-006] (PART 2). A fenced-code
   opener opens at or past its owner's content column and its lines move left
   by the opener's indentation (PART 0 S3, CARVE-P0-024). Carve has no
   indented-code-block construct. A closing run indented PAST its opener is
   not a delimiter but code content, which lets an indented ``` line appear
   as sample text inside a fence.
   The X->Carve converters re-base an indented Markdown fence to its
   container's content column. *)

backtick_fence = '`', '`', '`', {'`'} ;
tilde_fence = '~', '~', '~', {'~'} ;

code_fence_info = ( language_info, [space+, quoted_title], [space+, label] )
                | ( quoted_title, [space+, label] )
                | label ;
(* Fixed order: language, then header, then label. Both metadata slots
   are padding runs (CARVE-P7-002). Invalid info takes the invalid-fence
   fallback above. *)
language_info = (letter | digit | '-' | '_' | '+' | '#' | '.' | '/')+ ;  (* a single token; the punctuation covers real language tags like c++, c#, f#, asp.net, text/html. May start with a digit. The `/` is normative across all impls (so `text/html`, `image/svg` are language tokens, not split). A token MUST NOT start with `=`: a leading `=` is the raw_block opener (above), not a language. *)
label = '[', { character - ']' }, ']' ;  (* structured metadata, e.g. [Installation]; not part of the class *)
(* SEMANTIC CONSTRAINT: the slot ends at the MATCHING `]` -- the same balanced,
   escape- and LITERAL-SPAN-aware close `link_text` states (CARVE-P3-001), and
   for the same reason, since the slot holds an inline run (PART 9 §12). The
   class above is the context-free approximation and is right on every label
   with no nesting; it is not the close. So `[see [t](/u)]` is one label whose
   content is a link, a `]` inside a literal span is content, and an UNCLOSED
   backtick run takes the `]` into a verbatim span that runs past the line --
   which leaves the slot without a close, and the opener without its metadata,
   exactly as it leaves `[a `b](/u)` as prose rather than a link. Closing at
   the first `]` regardless of depth would select a nested link's closer, failing
   the opener's trailing-junk check. Not expressible context-free. *)
code_content = (* any text until matching fence, preserved literally *) ;
(* TABS IN CODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-007]. Literal tab characters in code content (fenced
   code blocks and inline code spans) are PRESERVED verbatim; a tab and N spaces
   are not interchangeable. Tab display width is a presentation concern (CSS
   `tab-size`). Tab-to-space expansion is NOT a default behavior -- it is opt-in
   via a tab-normalize extension (flat replacement, default 2 spaces, content
   only). Matches djot / CommonMark. *)

(* --- Blockquotes --- *)
blockquote = blockquote_line, {blockquote_line | lazy_continuation_line | continuation_marker_block}, [caption_slot] ;
(* marked and lazy lines may interleave: `> a` / lazy / `> b` is ONE quote.
   A `+` continuation_marker_block attaches a block at the quote's
   marker column -- see
   the CONTINUATION MARKER note below and PART 9 §17. *)
blockquote_line = quote_continuation_line | ('>', (newline | (space, inline_content, newline))) ;
quote_continuation_line = '>', { space, '>' }, space, continuation_marker ;
(* Every '>' takes one space, as on any quote line: `>+`, `>>+` and `>> +`
   are not quote lines and read as text, and `> >+` is a quote holding the
   text `>+`. *)

(* LAZY CONTINUATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-008] (CommonMark-compatible). After one or more
   blockquote_lines, a line that does NOT carry the '>' marker still continues
   the blockquote -- folding into the INNERMOST open paragraph, at whatever
   depth that paragraph sits -- provided:
     - a paragraph IS open at the quote's deepest frame (CARVE-P0-006,
       PART 0 S4); and the line is
     - not blank (a blank line ends the blockquote), and
     - not an interrupting opener in the selected owner's coordinates: a
       heading, table, fenced code opener (§10 I4), `:::` div, thematic break,
       or an invisible definition or comment recognized there ends the quote
       and starts outside it, and
     - not a caption ('^ ' ...), which attaches to the blockquote instead.
   A LIST MARKER (bullet or ordered) is NOT an exclusion: it folds into the
   open paragraph (PART 9 §10 I2, I6), so "> quoted \n - item" is ONE quote whose
   paragraph is "quoted\n- item". To put a real list inside a quote,
   `>`-prefix it or use the `+` continuation marker (PART 9 §17).
   Only PLAIN text and a folded list marker continue the open paragraph. The lazy line's text
   is appended to the blockquote's inner content before that content is
   block-parsed, so a hard-wrapped quoted paragraph need not repeat '>' on every
   line.
   CONTINUATION MARKER (PART 9 §17): to attach a real BLOCK (a list, fenced code,
   table, ...) to the quote without `>`-prefixing every line, put a lone `+` at
   the quote's marker column right after a quoted line; the following block at that column joins the
   quote body. This is the un-prefixed analogue of the list item's `+`. *)
lazy_continuation_line = inline_content, newline ;
(* SEMANTIC CONSTRAINT: matches only while a paragraph is open at the deepest
   frame (PART 0 S4), and only a line CARVE-P2-008 admits. *)

(* A GITHUB ALERT IMPORTS AS AN ADMONITION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-033]. A
   Markdown importer writes a GitHub alert as a Carve admonition. An alert is a
   blockquote that is a direct child of the document and whose first line,
   after the `>` marker and any spaces, is exactly one alert label: `[!`, one
   of the type names below in upper case, and `]`. A space or tab after the
   label that does not form a hard break is ignored. The type selects the
   admonition:

       NOTE       note
       TIP        tip
       IMPORTANT  info
       WARNING    warning
       CAUTION    danger

   The importer drops the label line and imports the rest of the quote, lazy
   continuation lines included, as the admonition's body. It writes no title:
   the type carries the meaning. The migration report records each conversion
   as `markdown-github-alert` with `normalized` fidelity. So

       > [!WARNING]
       > Back up first.

   imports as

       ::: warning
       Back up first.
       :::

   Every other blockquote stays a blockquote, its label kept as text:
     - a label line that carries more text, a hard break, or an escaped
       bracket (`\[!NOTE]`);
     - a label in lower or mixed case (`[!note]`, `[!Note]`);
     - a bracketed label outside the five (`[!DANGER]`, `[! NOTE]`);
     - a label that is not on the quote's first line;
     - a label with nothing after it, blank quote lines not counting as a body;
     - a quote nested in a list item, a footnote, another blockquote or an
       alert's body.
   `github-alert-marker` keeps reporting the label in those quotes. *)

(* --- Lists --- *)
list = unordered_list | ordered_list | definition_list ;

unordered_list = unordered_item+ ;
unordered_item = bullet_marker, [item_attributes], space, [task_marker], list_item_content ;
(* the optional task_marker makes the item a TASK item (checkbox); the
   plain-vs-task axis is a PART 9 §11 same-list criterion *)
bullet_marker = '-' | '*' ;  (* `+` is NOT a bullet in Carve -- it is the list
                                continuation marker (PART 9 §17); a `+ ` line is
                                ordinary paragraph text. Deviates from djot. *)
(* MARKER REQUIRES CONTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-009]. A marker that takes a separator space
   opens its block only when followed by that space AND non-empty content.
   The separator is SPACES ONLY (CARVE-P7-002). Content is any character
   outside the `' ' | '\t'` run that NO TRAILING WHITESPACE drops
   (CARVE-P2-025). A content-less marker line, bare (`-`) or with trailing
   whitespace only (`- `, `-   `), is paragraph text. Stricter than
   CommonMark, which treats a bare `-` as an empty item. The rule holds for
   EVERY such marker: bullets, ordered markers, the definition-term marker
   `::` and the description marker `:`. The three clauses below apply it.

   THE DESCRIPTION MARKER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-026]. A `:` line carrying only
   whitespace opens no description. It is a plain line under the open term,
   which folds it as a soft break: `:`, `: `, `:   ` and `:` + TAB are one
   document.

   A LEADING CONTENT TAB FOLLOWS NORMAL INDENTATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-010].
   With the separator space present and content after it, a tab that begins
   the content is ordinary leading whitespace and is stripped: content_column
   is where the content actually STARTS (PART 9 §24 C3). `::` + space + TAB +
   `x` renders `<dl><dt>x</dt></dl>`. TABS IN CODE (CARVE-P2-007) covers code
   content only.

   WHICH CHARACTERS THE CONTENT TEST IGNORES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-027]. A tab
   PAST the separator run is judged by the content test: a body of nothing but
   tabs opens nothing, so `: ` + TAB folds into the term, while `: ` + TAB +
   `x` opens a description. Every character outside `' ' | '\t'` is content:
   `: ` + VT and `: ` + NBSP each open a description. *)

ordered_list = ordered_item+ ;
ordered_item = ordered_marker, [item_attributes], space, list_item_content ;
(* The first item fixes the dialect (decimal / alpha / roman) and the
   delimiter (`.` or `)`); classification and the ambiguous-letter
   tie-break are in PART 9 §11. Whether a marker line interrupts a paragraph is
   PART 9 §10 I2.
   The two delimiters are the trailing `.` and `)` only. djot's
   parenthesized `(1)` / `(a)` form is INTENTIONALLY NOT a marker -- it is
   too easily confused with a prose parenthetical -- so `(1) text` stays
   literal paragraph text. *)
ordered_marker = (digit+ | letter | roman_numeral), ('.' | ')')
               | '.' ;
(* A BARE DOT OPENS A DECIMAL LIST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-011] (Carve addition; no Djot or CommonMark equivalent).
   The VALUE may be omitted when the delimiter is `.`: a bare `. ` is a decimal
   ordered marker counting from 1, the AsciiDoc-style shorthand.

     . first          -> <ol><li>first</li><li>second</li></ol>
     . second

   ONLY `.` MAY DROP ITS VALUE. `) text` stays paragraph text: a leading `) `
   collides with prose parentheticals far more often than a leading `. ` does,
   which is the same asymmetry that keeps `(1)` from being a marker.

   IT IS A SPELLING, NOT A DIALECT. The bare dot IS decimal-dot, so it opens
   and continues one list with the explicit form -- `. a` then `2. b` is one
   `<ol>`, and so is `1. a` then `. b`. It carries no value, so it cannot set a
   start: the `<ol>` omits `start` and `type`, and `3.` is how a start is
   written. li-attributes attach exactly as they do to every other marker
   (`.{#x} text`), because the shape is marker, [attributes], space, content
   and the block sits BEFORE the required space rather than competing with it.

   A BARE DOT AFTER A BLANK LINE OPENS A LIST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-012]. After a blank line a paragraph beginning `. ` opens a
   list; so does one beginning with `.{` plus a
   valid attribute block and a space. Escape the marker (`\. text`) to keep the
   line prose.

   AUTHORED FORM (PART 11 §6). An implementation MUST record the list-marker
   spelling so the writer reproduces it. Ordered list numbers are
   canonicalized on output. *)
roman_numeral = ('i' | 'v' | 'x' | 'l' | 'c' | 'd' | 'm')+
              | ('I' | 'V' | 'X' | 'L' | 'C' | 'D' | 'M')+ ;

(* LIST-ITEM ATTRIBUTES (Carve addition; NORMATIVE -- extends PART 9 §15). An
   attribute block ABUTTING the marker (no space between marker and `{`)
   attaches its attributes to the `<li>` itself; the marker's required space
   follows the block:
     `-{.c} text`     -> `<li class="c">text</li>`
     `3.{#x k=v} text`-> `<li id="x" k="v">text</li>`
   For task items the block abuts the marker, before the task marker:
     `-{.c} [ ] text` -> `<li class="c"><input ...> text</li>`.
   WHITESPACE IS THE DISCRIMINATOR -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-013]:
   - `-{.c} text` (attr ABUTS marker) -> the `{.c}` is part of the MARKER and
     attributes the `<li>`. This is the ONLY way to attribute the `<li>`.
   - `- {.c} text` (a space BEFORE `{`) -> the `{.c}` is ordinary item CONTENT,
     NOT a li-attribute. It then follows the normal inline/block rules: a
     same-line `{.c}` is leading inline content; a `{.c}` alone on its own line
     (inside the item) is a block-attribute line that floats to the next block
     within the item -- e.g. a `{.blue}` line preceding an indented `> quote`
     puts `.blue` on the `<blockquote>` (PART 9 §15), NOT on the `<li>`. This keeps
     li-attributes and block-attributes cleanly separated.
   - DISAMBIGUATION of the abutting block mirrors the inline-span rule (CARVE-P9-020):
     it is consumed as li-attributes only if it yields >= 1 attribute (`#id`,
     `.class`, `key=value`) OR is the blessed empty block (`-{} text` -> bare
     `<li>`). Otherwise (`-{+a+}`, `-{not attrs}`) the `-{` is not a marker and
     the line stays ordinary paragraph/inline text.
   - `-{` and `1.{` / `1){` are not list markers: each requires a space.
   - A trailing `{…}` line folded onto a tight item is not a
     list-item attribute. *)
item_attributes = attributes ;

list_item_content = first_block_content
                  | (inline_content, newline, {list_continuation | nested_list | continuation_marker_block}) ;
list_continuation = indent, inline_content, newline ;
nested_list = indent, list ;
indent = whitespace, {whitespace} ;
(* This production IS a leading indentation run, so its column arithmetic is
   A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE LEADING RUN (CARVE-P7-001); how much indent nests what
   is NOT a fixed character count -- the content-column rules are PART 9 §24. *)
(* MARKER-LINE NESTED LIST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-014]. A sub-list whose first marker sits on
   the PARENT item's marker line (`- - A`) is an ordinary persistent nested_list,
   identical to writing the sub-marker on its own indented line. It is NOT a
   one-off lone item: following markers at the sub-list's content column MERGE
   into the same nested list, and a post-blank block indented to that column is
   a further block of the open nested item (it reaches its content column). This matches
   reference djot.js (`@djot/djot`) and CommonMark. *)
(* Continuation marker (PART 9 §17): a lone `+` at an open container's marker column,
   or directly after a quote prefix, attaches one block starting at that
   column. A first-block `+` after the last opener on its line attaches to
   that innermost opener when a non-list block starts at column 0 of its quote
   level (the document's, or an enclosing quote's content) or at the opener
   line's own column. At an open list item's marker column, a following
   same-column list item is a sibling, so the `+` is ordinary text and the
   refused-marker lint reports it. *)
continuation_marker = '+', newline ;
(* CARVE-P7-001 item 5 removes a line-final space/tab run before this
   production is matched, so that source spelling remains a marker. A
   vertical tab or form feed is not a marker separator. *)
continuation_marker_block = continuation_marker, block ;
(* First-block form (Carve addition; see PART 9 §17 L4): a lone `+` as the
   sole content right after a container's last opener (`- +`, `1. +`, `: +`,
   `> +`, `[^a]: +`, `- - +`) opens a body that is the one block starting at
   column 0 of its quote level or that opener line's column, with no inline lead;
   a list item, footnote body or description body also takes its next owned
   block at or past its content column (PART 9 §17 L4, CARVE-P9-067);
   a list item at a column owned by an open list remains a sibling.
   `- + text` keeps `+ text` as literal
   inline content -- only a bare `+` triggers this. With no block at that column
   after it, `> +` and `[^a]: +` keep the `+` as text. *)
(* One block, exactly as `continuation_marker_block` above: the two forms
   differ in WHERE the marker sits, never in how much it takes. *)
first_block_content = continuation_marker, block ;

(* Task lists are unordered lists with special markers *)
task_marker = '[', task_state, ']', space ;
(* `x`/`X` render a CHECKED checkbox; every other state (` `, `_`,
   `-`, `/`, `!`, `>`, `?`) renders an UNCHECKED checkbox. `_` is an
   alternative spelling for the open state ` `. *)
task_state = ' ' | 'x' | 'X' | '_' | '-' | '/' | '!' | '>' | '?' ;
(* THE STATE IS RECORDED, NOT ONLY THE BOX. `[-]`, `[/]`, `[!]`, `[>]` and `[?]`
   are distinguishable states an author chose between, so PART 12 records the
   character in `list_item.taskState` and CARVE-P11-041 writes it back.
   `[_]` is an open-state alias whose spelling is retained. `checked` drives
   the rendering above; the state is the author's spelling,
   as `bulletChar` is for `-` vs `*`.

   TASK STATE IS NOT A SAME-LIST AXIS. PART 9 §11's criteria are unchanged: a `[ ]`
   item and a `[-]` item are the SAME list, where a `-` bullet and a `*`
   bullet are not. The marker character splits a list; the state inside the
   box does not. *)
(* THE CHECKBOX IS A PROPERTY OF THE ITEM, NOT OF ITS FIRST BLOCK. It is
   written directly after the `<li>` opener whatever the marker line goes on to
   open, and nothing about that block reaches it. Only the CONTENT moves: it
   sits beside the checkbox when the first block renders inline, and on its own
   indented line below it when it does not.
     `- [ ] a`        -> `<li><input ...> a</li>`
     `- [ ] > q`      -> `<li><input ...> ` newline `  <blockquote> ...`
   A quote, a code fence, a `:::` div, a table row, a heading and a thematic
   break as the first block each still get the checkbox.

   THE TASK BOX CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-015]. The box is an
   <input type="checkbox"> and the word beside it is a SIBLING TEXT NODE - not
   a <label>, not an aria-label - so without a name a reader meets an
   unlabeled disabled checkbox. The box takes an `aria-label` naming it with
   the item's own visible text: the DERIVED TEXT of the item's first block
   (PART 9R R4's derivation). An unresolved reference image contributes its
   alt as written, as it does to a heading id: `- [ ] ![x][r]` is named `x`,
   and `- [ ] ![/x/ y][r] z` is named `/x/ y z`. A name is ONE line, so each
   run of ASCII whitespace in the derived text collapses to a single space and
   the result is trimmed; a NO-BREAK space is content and survives.
     `- [ ] unchecked`   ->  `<input type="checkbox" disabled
                              aria-label="unchecked">`
     `- [x] done with `c``  ->  `... checked disabled aria-label="done with c"`
   NO NAME IS WRITTEN WHERE THERE IS NOTHING TO SAY. Only the item's FIRST
   block can sit beside the box, and only a paragraph carries inline text, so
   an item whose first block is a quote, a fence, a `:::` div, a table, a
   heading or a thematic break takes NO `aria-label` - as does one whose first
   paragraph derives to the empty string. The checkbox and its state are still
   written in every one of those leads, which is the rule directly above. *)

(* --- Definition Lists --- *)
definition_list = definition_entry+ ;
definition_entry = definition_term+, [blank_line], definition_body,
                   {[blank_line], definition_body} ;
(* A blank line may separate a term from its definition, and one definition
   from the next, for readability -- the blank is a separator only (djot
   parity). A blank NOT followed by a `: ` definition ends the entry. *)
definition_term = "::", space, inline_content, newline, {term_continuation_line} ;
term_continuation_line = inline_content, newline ;
(* A term folds a following plain line as a soft break; a blank line, a new
   `::`/`: ` marker, or anything else that ends a paragraph ends it. A term
   holds inline content only -- no block body. Unlike a heading, a term KEEPS
   its fold. It has no ownership of its own (PART 0 FAMILIES, CARVE-P0-024):
   an opener under an open term, at or past the owner's content column, ends
   the term and opens in that owner, and a following unprefixed plain line
   folds into the term as into an open paragraph (S4). *)
definition_body = (':', definition_separator, first_block_content)
                | (':', definition_separator, inline_content, newline,
                   { definition_continuation
                   | (blank_line+, definition_continuation)
                   | continuation_marker_block
                   | lazy_continuation_line }) ;
definition_separator = space, {space} ;
(* FIRST-BLOCK (`: +`): when the sole content is a lone `+`, the body is the
   following block at the container's marker column -- the definition-list twin
   of the list-item `- +` opener (`first_block_content`). `: \+` is a literal
   `+`. *)

(* SEPARATOR WIDTH SETS THE BODY'S COLUMN. The separator is a run
   of ONE OR MORE spaces, and the body's content column is `1 + separator
   width`: `: x` establishes column 2, `:  x` column 3, `:    x` column 5. A
   continuation line qualifies by REACHING its own body's column (PART 9 §24
   C1), so the spellings differ only in how far their continuations indent,
   never in what they mean.

   ONE SPACE IS CANONICAL. A wider separator is accepted and the formatter
   narrows it, which is what a formatter is for; nothing about the document's
   meaning depends on the author having picked the canonical width. Narrowing
   the separator narrows the body's column, so a canonical rewrite carries every
   continuation inside that body down by the same amount. A formatter that
   trims the separator and leaves the body where it was changes what the
   document says. *)
definition_continuation = (definition_indent, inline_content, newline) ;
definition_indent = whitespace, {whitespace} ;
(* DEFINITION SEPARATOR SETS THE BODY COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-016].
   The required `: ` marker may have a wider run of separator spaces. Its
   content column is the marker column plus one colon and that run's visual
   width. A formatter narrowing the run moves continuation indentation by
   the same number of columns to preserve ownership. C1 measures tabs and
   C3 (CARVE-P9-083) applies the shared reach and extent tests.

   The definition body's `: ` marker selects the shared marker-body rule in
   PART 9 §24 C3 (CARVE-P9-083). Its separator width sets the content column;
   its child blocks, blank runs, lazy paragraph lines, `+` attachment and
   closing by column follow the list-item procedure. The `::` term remains an
   inline leaf, not another marker body. A recognized opener at or past the
   body's column begins a child there. A line below it is classified by the
   same owner and interruption tests as a line below an item column.

   AUTHORED BLOCK BASES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-018]. Definition
   bodies follow PART 0 S3 (CARVE-P0-024) at the content column fixed by their
   separator: an opener past it opens in the body, measured from its own
   line. This construct adds no rule of its own.

   A NESTED CONTAINER DOES NOT WITHHOLD THE COLUMNS IT DOES NOT REACH.
   A list or a body opened inside the `dd` has a content column
   of its own, and a definition written between the `dd`'s column and that one
   is the `dd`'s: PART 0 S2 selects the innermost container whose content
   column the line REACHES, and a container it does not reach neither owns
   the line nor withholds it (CARVE-P0-020). `:: t` / `:  - a` over four
   spaces registers, exactly as `- - x` over three does.

   Thus the `>` on

       :: t
       : body
          > q

   opens a block quote. The same rule covers headings, lists, tables, code/raw/
   colon fences, definitions, attributes followed by their target and comments,
   with or without a preceding blank. Tabs participate by visual column. An
   ordinary over-indented line which is not a recognized opener remains
   paragraph text, and the leading run is measured once: implementations MUST
   NOT probe every possible dedent. Opaque payload indentation beyond the
   opener's own is authored data, and a closer is measured like its opener.

   A diagnostic for over-indented literal text that begins with a block opener
   SHOULD offer two fixes: dedent for structural intent, or escape the opener
   to keep literal text. *)
(* The term marker is TWO colons. A single-colon `: term` line is NOT a
   definition list -- it is ordinary paragraph text. (Deliberate: djot uses a
   single colon, but `::` keeps the term marker distinct from the `:::` div
   fence and from a stray `:` in prose.)

   The body uses PART 9 §24 C3 (CARVE-P9-083). Its distinct syntax is the
   `: ` marker and its distinct output is `<dd>`. The `::` term and entry
   separator remain governed by this definition-list production. *)
